> Reproduction evidence and limits

The original program's ten archives contain 919949 values. Native delegation reproduces
all dtypes, shapes and value bytes, both TSV files, all three original PNGs and non-timing
JSON values exactly. The report repeats those comparisons; training duration is remeasured.
The independent NumPy checker reads prepared raw data and artifacts without datamunge:
it checks 749714 forward values, 53472 isolated source/class G derivatives, 24 scalar finite
differences, seeded noise and all 3000 minibatch source rows. This is separate numerical
validation, not a second fitted benchmark or proof of GAN convergence.

The compiled API examples and native/Python/R component checks cover initialization order,
label validation before leaf creation, stable extreme logits, first/higher continuous
paths, strides, owned result lifetimes and selected-store Adam writes. The report's complete
experiment remains the C++ loop above; host-language component coverage does not claim
completed independent Python/R experiment ports.

This single small run has no seed sweep, external quality metric or confidence interval.
Fixed noise enables comparisons across checkpoints and controls, but remains one finite
sample. D's source accuracy, auxiliary class accuracy and the coarse MMD answer distinct
questions. The visible grey samples, low requested-class accuracy and stronger repeated-mean
coarse baseline remain part of the result.

References: [Goodfellow et al. 2014](https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.2661) introduces the GAN
and non-saturating source alternative; [Odena, Olah and Shlens 2017](https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.09585)
introduces auxiliary-class conditioning. Architecture, seeds, grouping, schedule and artifacts
here follow the repository's original compact teaching program.
